// prelude.schema.haus: the types that are always in scope. Deliberately
// small: only what nearly every schema needs. Everything else lives in the
// standard module std/time or in schema packages (@haus/money, …), imported
// explicitly by the schema (spec §13).
//
// None of these are keywords. `True` is an ordinary tag, and the data grammar
// has no reserved words at all. The prelude only fixes what these tags MEAN,
// so every language binding can map them to its native type.

// ── Core variants ────────────────────────────────────────────
type Bool       = closed < False | True >
type Maybe a    = closed < None | Some a >      // always written; a missing field is not None (see below)
type Result e a = closed < Err e | Ok a >

// ── Optional fields and Maybe ────────────────────────────────
// Two separate things (spec §1):
//   name?: T        the field may be missing ("not given"); never None
//   name: Maybe T   the value is always written: None or Some(…)
//   name?: Maybe T  not given, None or Some(…): a partial update that can
//                   leave, clear or set a value

// ── Built-in types ───────────────────────────────────────────
// One per kind of value, plus the numeric representations:
//
//   String                 a string
//   Bytes                  a byte string: x"0b34ff"
//   Array a                an ordered list
//   Map v                  a record used as a dictionary: any string keys,
//                          values of one type: { en: "Hello", "pt-BR": "Olá" }
//   { name: T, … }         a record with known fields
//
// Records and variants are open: a reader ignores fields it doesn't know and
// keeps unknown tags as Unknown values. `closed { … }` and `closed < … >`
// forbid anything not listed. `< >` is a variant with any tags.
//
// Number literals are exact decimals: the parser keeps the literal as written,
// including trailing zeros. The schema picks the native representation:
//
//   Int                    arbitrary-precision integer   (BigInt)
//   Int8 .. Int64          bounded integers, out-of-range is an error
//   UInt8 .. UInt64
//   Float32, Float64       binary floating point, rounding is accepted;
//                          also accept the tags NaN, Infinity, NegInfinity
//   Decimal                exact decimal, scale preserved (19.90 != 19.9)
//
// `Int` decodes to an arbitrary-size integer in every binding; for a native
// fixed-size integer, write `Int32`, `Int64`, ….
//
// A schemaless decoder must not lose precision or reject large numbers (spec §3):
// every integer becomes an arbitrary-size integer (bigint in JavaScript), never
// a type chosen by looking at the data, so types don't depend on the document
// (a stream reader can't look ahead). Other numbers become exact decimals or
// the literal text, never a lossy float.

// ── Streams ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
// `Stream a` describes a whole file as a sequence of `a`s, rather than a
// single value. It is only valid as the root type of a file.
//   type Events = Stream AppEvent
